      "term": "Political fragmentation",
      "matchText": "Political fragmentation",
      "definition": "The absence of a unifying empire \u2014 Europe in the early-modern period was split among hundreds of competing principalities, kingdoms, and city-states with overlapping jurisdictions. A book or person banned in one could move to another; no single bureaucracy could suppress an idea everywhere at once. Historians of science treat this fragmentation as one of the strongest candidate explanations for why modern science erupted in Europe rather than in the much larger, more politically unified civilizations of China or the Islamic world.",
